abduco
Usage: abduco [-a|-A|-c|-n] [-r] [-l] [-f] [-e detachkey] name command

Detach a program from the terminal that started it. The program keeps its
own pseudo-terminal, owned by abduco, so it survives the terminal going
away -- a closed console, a dropped serial line, a killed ssh session.

	-c	create a session and attach to it
	-n	create a session and leave it detached
	-a	attach to an existing session
	-A	attach if it exists, create it if it does not
	-e	set the detach key (default is ctrl-\)
	-r	attach read-only -- watch without being able to type
	-f	force, even when the session looks already attached
	-l	force the created session to be a login shell

	abduco -c work			start a session called work
	ctrl-\				detach; the program keeps running
	abduco				list sessions
	abduco -a work			pick it back up
	abduco -r work			watch it without touching it

With no command, /bin/sh is started. Several people may attach to one
session at once and all see the same screen, so the console and a remote
session can watch the same work.

Sessions are unix sockets under $HOME, which is tmpfs here -- so they do
not survive a reboot.

See also: setsid, cttyhack, tty
